    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d534">

                <pb facs="0655" n="547" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg547"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foster to Prince Cantacuzène.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-02-16"><hi rendition="#italic">February 16,
                        1893</hi>.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir</hi>: The Department is advised that the
                    consul of Russia at New York City has peremptorily refused to visé a passport,
                    No. 46250, issued on the 6th instant to Mannie Lerin, a duly naturalized citizen
                    of the United States, born at Odessa, Russia. Miss Lerin explains that she
                    desires to visit her parents in Russia, and the action of the consul, of course,
                    precludes all possibility other doing so.</p>
                <p>It is inferred that the consul bases his action upon the general instructions of
                    his Government, allowing him to decline to vise a passport of a former subject
                    of Russia who had left his native land without permission to escape military
                    service. If this theory is correct it is not perceived how this condition can
                    apply to the case of a woman, and I shall be glad to learn, if you please, the
                    reason of the consul’s action in this particular instance.</p>
                <p>Awaiting, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John W. Foster.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
